`requirementEdgeLabelBackground` is a theme variable for edge label backgrounds and is semantically unrelated to whether `nodeStyles` should be applied to node paths. Using it as a guard here means: if a theme defines `requirementEdgeLabelBackground` but has no `borderColorArray`, node path styles are still applied; if a theme defines neither, node path styles are silently skipped even when `nodeStyles` is non-empty (e.g. from a user `style` statement). A cleaner condition that expresses the actual intent would be:

```ts
if (nodeStyles && node.look !== 'handDrawn') {
  shapeSvg.selectAll('.outer-path path').attr('style', nodeStyles);
}
```

`genColor` calls `configApi.getConfig()` internally, but `getStyles` also calls `configApi.getConfig()` immediately before invoking `genColor(options)` (line 31). This results in two `getConfig()` calls per `getStyles` invocation. Consider passing the already-fetched `config` (or just `look` and `themeVariables`) as a parameter to `genColor` to avoid the redundant lookup:

```js
const genColor = (options, { look, themeVariables }) => { ... };
// in getStyles:
return `${genColor(options, { look, themeVariables })}...`;
```

Building `diagram` with `+=` inside a `forEach` loop is an O(n²) string-concatenation pattern. For the small test data here it has no measurable impact, but the idiomatic alternative is to collect parts in an array and join at the end:

```ts
const parts: string[] = [`requirementDiagram\n`];
relationshipTypes.forEach((_rel, i) => {
  parts.push(`    requirement req_${i} { ... }\n`);
  parts.push(`    element elem_${i} { ... }\n`);
});
const diagram = parts.join('');
```
This also makes the intent clearer.
